# Code documentation rule

Full standard: `docs/framework/10-documentation.md` → "Code-level
documentation standards". Adjust the `globs` above to the project's real
source layout once the stack is fixed.

## Module / file header

Every new public module or file begins with a short header comment in the
language's idiom:

- One line: what this module is responsible for.
- `REQ-<id>` that introduced it (and later ones that materially changed it).
- Path to the feature doc: `docs/features/REQ-<id>-<slug>/feature-doc.md`.

## Public API doc comments

Every exported / public function, class, method, type, and constant gets a
doc comment covering: purpose, parameters (with units and valid ranges),
return value, errors/exceptions raised, and any non-obvious behaviour
(ordering, idempotency, side effects, performance characteristics). Include a
short example when the calling convention is not obvious.

Private helpers need a doc comment only when the name does not fully say
what they do.

## Comments

- Explain **why**: business rules (`per FR-3`), workarounds (`ledger API
  rejects batches > 500 — OQ-7`), ordering constraints, performance choices.
- Do not narrate what the code does (`// loop over items`, `// return
  result`). Do not describe the change you are making.
- `TODO` / `FIXME` / `HACK` must carry a reference: `TODO(OQ-12): …` or
  `TODO(#345): …`. Bare ones are rejected by Bugbot and CI.

## Configuration, flags, environment variables

Where a config key / flag / env var is read, add a one-line comment with its
purpose and default, and make sure it is listed in `feature-doc.md` §5.

## Dependencies

A new third-party dependency requires a why-sentence in `feature-doc.md` §8
and a line in `technical-plan.md` §3.

## Logging

Log lines at boundaries include a stable feature identifier (the `REQ-` ID
or feature tag) so production issues trace back to the requirement.
